  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "+eHR4DyV641JOf0d1VsZkocy21k=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:05.410Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The flag on PinkRoccade puts the verdict plainly: the verticals are ideal but the business model is product, not custom. Healthcare and municipalities are exactly the buyers the pilot wants to reach, and the firm's platforms are its own vertical products.\n\nA product company and a custom-build partner monetise different things \u2014 a roadmap versus delivery hours \u2014 and only the second has a commercial reason to place someone else's component in front of a client. That is why sector fit alone cannot rescue the row, and it is the same conclusion the Portuguese healthtech row for Glintt reaches independently.\n\nThe second half of the flag is a data-quality warning: all financials are stale by fifteen years, including the recorded revenue of 197 million. Any sizing judgement built on that figure is unsupported. It joins Valcon's pre-merger revenue and Sopra Steria Netherlands' post-merger figures as a recurring problem in the Dutch rows \u2014 the revenue column is present far more often than it is current.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- PinkRoccade (Healthcare / Local Gov) (NL)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Healthcare, municipalities \u00b7 NL profile: healthcare, public \u00b7 rev 197000000 \u00b7 All financials stale by 15 years. Verticals are ideal but the business model is product, not custom.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:32:05.410226Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:05.410231Z"
    }
  },
